---
description: Documentation rules for CircleSfera — documentation lives in Notion, the repo is evidence
globs: docs/**, CHANGELOG.md, README.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, AGENTS.md
alwaysApply: false
---

All CircleSfera documentation lives in Notion → CircleSfera Documentation (links in `AGENTS.md`),
written in English. The repository holds code, schema, tests and configuration — evidence, not
documentation.

- Do not add documentation files to the repository. The only exception is the full text of a new
  Architecture Decision Record in `docs/adr/`, indexed in Notion → Architecture Decisions.
- Follow Notion → Governance: Documentation State Model (NORMATIVE / IMPLEMENTED / VERIFIED /
  PLANNED / EXPLORATORY / DEPRECATED / UNKNOWN), Source of Truth Registry, Documentation Standards,
  Canonical Terminology.
- When the system and the documentation disagree, classify the conflict before fixing either side:
  documentation drift → correct the documentation; implementation drift → candidate defect, do not
  rewrite documentation to legitimize the code; decision conflict → stop and escalate; insufficient
  evidence → UNKNOWN. Record it in the Conflict & Decision Register — never resolve it silently.
- Verify every factual statement against the canonical artifact: `schema.prisma` for data,
  controllers for endpoints, the owning service for business rules, `circlesfera-frontend/src/index.css`
  for tokens, workflows and compose files for deployment, `package.json` for versions.
- Code shows what is implemented, not what is intended. A test proves only its own scope.
- Never document an endpoint, model, enum or flow that does not exist in code.
- Cross-references use plain section numbers (`section 9.4`) — never the section symbol.
- Product name is always **CircleSfera**.
- `CHANGELOG.md` follows Keep a Changelog; user- and operator-visible changes go under `[Unreleased]`.
- `circlesfera-landing/` was removed (Jul 2026). Do not document it as active or restore it.
